%% The definition of a Noweb file, given by Ramsey, is simply a file
%% containing one or more chunks; minimally, a Noweb file will contain the
%% default documentation chunk.

%% To summarize this section, since it is longer than the previous section,
%% the object is to convert the noweb document to tool syntax and parse it
%% with the peg parser.

%% In more explicit words, this section describes the actions that occur
%% when a user invokes [[whyse]] interactively (with \textit{M-x}) and the
%% preconditions have been met; the [[whyse]] function has already been
%% introduced, and only the ``meaty'' business end of its operation has
%% been left undefined until now. Ergo, [[w-with-project]] gathers together
%% the functionality that converts a Noweb to its tool syntax with a
%% project's specified shell script, and parses the text before the next
%% section of body forms is executed. Those send the parsed text to the
%% database, and finally create the atomic window for the IDE in the active
%% frame.

%% In earlier development versions the following function body was
%% referenced with noweb chunks rather than being defined as a function,
%% however it was decided that implementing this as a function allowed a
%% user-suggestion for an API-like function that was planned for
%% implementation anyways. Now it is a function, satisfying internal and
%% API needs.

<<parse-project-in-temp-buffer>>=
(with-temp-buffer
  (insert (shell-command-to-string (w-project-script project)))
  (goto-char (point-min))
  (w--parse-current-buffer-with-rules))

<<with-project>>=
;; TODO: understand how and why this macro works like
;; `with-temp-buffer'; I only copied a couple things from the defintion
;; of that macro, but I don't yet understand its definition fully. I
;; will need to before I really start to understand Emacs Lisp. I do
;; understand enough, but there is a lot more to know. A whole lot.
(defmacro w-with-project (project &rest body)
  (declare (indent 0) (indent 2) (debug t))
  "Evaluates BODY with PROJECT in scope. This is like `let' except
it doesn't bind arbitrary values to arbitrary symbols. PROJECT is
taken as the symbol `project' during evaluation of BODY.

PROJECT is in scope because it is an argument of this macro. This
may or may not work as expected."
  (eval `(progn ,@body)))

<<buffer parsing function>>=
;; FIXME: the current parse tree contains a `nil' after the chunk type
;; and number assoc, and that needs to be analyzed. Why is this `nil' in
;; the stack? I assume and believe it is because of the collapsing of
;; stringy tokens; when a token should be put back onto the stack it may
;; also be putting a `nil' onto the stack in the first call to the
;; function.
;;;; Parsing expression grammar (PEG) rules
(defun w--parse-current-buffer-with-rules ()
  "Parse the current buffer with the PEG defined for Noweb tool syntax."
  (with-peg-rules (<<PEG rules>>)

   ;; FIXME: within this local, temporary scope there is no reason to use `w--' in a name.
   (let (w--peg-parser-within-codep
         (w--first-stringy-token? t))
     (peg-run (peg noweb) #'w--parse-failure-function))))

(defun w--parse-failure-function (lst)
  (setq w--parse-success nil)
  (pop-to-buffer (clone-buffer))
  (save-excursion
    (put-text-property (point) (point-min)
                       'face 'success)

    (put-text-property (point) (point-max)
                       'face 'error)

    (goto-char (point-max))
    (message "PEXes which failed:\n%S" lst)))
@

<<Customization and global variables>>=
(defvar w--parse-success t
  "The success or failure of the last parsing of noweb tool syntax.")
@




\part{Discovering the mystery of the meat}
\label{part:mystery-meat}

What are the steps to follow to implement \whyse{}? What is the meat of
the application?

The application has been in development for a little over two years and
I haven't made progress in nearly that much time. I know that I need
{an~event~loop~\ref{event-hook-loop}}.

What are the individual events that take place in a system such as
\whyse{}? What takes place between the input of a file in \noweb{} format
into an application that either extends the mode(s) suitable for that
format or extends the mode(s) suitable for the resulting \LaTeX{}-format
file?

  The standard steps in the usage of \noweb{} are as follows:

  \begin{itemize}[noweb-to-latex]
  \item Write a \noweb{}-formatted file, and
  \item Call \notangle on the file, with various options, OR
  \item Call \noweave on the file, with various options.
  \end{itemize}

  with not much else to it (except in expert cases, like the writing of a
  large book or the development of an application like \whyse{}).

Developing an application like this, even when a rudimentary design is
available in \textsc{WHS} described in CITE brown1991% \cite[1991 by Brown and Czejdo]{brown1991}
, is not easy. I haven't fully designed the program,
or completed an analysis: I suppose I'm taking an ``agile'' approach to
development. It's certainly not ``test-driven'' or following a
traditional software development lifecycle (SDLC).

The following quotation examples the output of \noweave. The in-line
forms [[@<<chunk-name>>]] and [[some prose with a [[quotation]]]] are
not exampled below, only a code chunk. Documentation chunks are literal
\LaTeX{}, with minimal embracement by \noweb{} macros (just
\texttt{\\nwbegindocs{*}}).

\begin{Verbatim}
\nwbegincode{97}
  \sublabel{NW43JrUv-3RJbFb-1}
  \nwmargintag{{\nwtagstyle{}\subpageref{NW43JrUv-3RJbFb-1}}}
  \moddef{Commentary~{\nwtagstyle{}\subpageref{NW43JrUv-3RJbFb-1}}}\endmoddef
  \nwstartdeflinemarkup
    \nwusesondefline{\\{NW43JrUv-1gS5zu-1}}
  \nwenddeflinemarkup
;;; Commentary:
;; WHYSE was described by Brown and Czedjo in _A Hypertext for Literate
;; Programming_ (1991).
;;
;; Brown, M., Czejdo, B. (1991). A hypertext for literate programming. In: Akl,
;;    S.G., Fiala, F., Koczkodaj, W.W. (eds) Advances in Computing and
;;    Information — ICCI '90. ICCI 1990. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol
;;    468. Springer, Berlin, Heidelberg.
;;    https://doi-org.libproxy.mtroyal.ca/10.1007/3-540-53504-7_82.
;;
;; A paper describing this implementation---written in Noweb and browsable,
;; editable, and auditable with WHYSE, or readable in the printed form---is
;; hoped to be submitted to The Journal of Open Source Software (JOSS) before
;; the year 2024. N.B.: the paper will include historical information about
;; literate programming, and citations (especially of those given credit here
;; for ideating WHYSE itself).

\nwused{\\{NW43JrUv-1gS5zu-1}}
\nwendcode{}
\end{Verbatim}

The fact that \noweb{} users usually author documents LaTeX documentation,
rather than nroff or HTML, gives quarter to the existing \LaTeX{}
support in \emacs{}. \reftex and \auctex support extensions as a matter of
course because they're written in \elisp{}; it's part of what they are.
Could I be rid of the \whyse{} project (at least a first implementation)
by beginning with adding support for the \noweb{} cross-reference, label,
and index macros? It's very likely. It's what I'm doing.

My development plan is to add support for \noweb{}'s macros to \reftex and
\auctex then use the information gleaned from these packages to
buttonize and markup (with overlays) the chunk headers and
cross-references in poly-noweb buffers. \texttt{diff}s of the
continually watched product of the \noweb{}-formatted source file and the
user-modified \LaTeX{}-formatted document (which is a derivation of the
former), and suggestions for filters could conceivably allow two-way
communication between \noweb{} and \auctex. Would that be \whyse{}? I don't
think it would be wise to call it \whyse{}---the \textsc{WHS} implemented
in \emacs{}---but it would still be \whyse{}: \emph{a particular}
\textsc{((no)Web~HYpertext~System~in~Emacs)}. It may still be a stepping
stone to the original notion, which would be interesting to explore, but
I think it would be better than that original goal as well; it wouldn't
be as flexible as the \noweb{} system is, however, as it would only
support \LaTeX{} documentation, whereas \noweb{} supports other backends.

\begin{quote}
  \reftex has been bundled and pre-installed with \emacs{} since version
  20.2.

  --- the \reftex \texinfo manual, ``with typographic editions''.
\end{quote}

Given \reftex has been a part of \emacs{} for so long it makes sense to
simply defer to its featureset where it provides something useful,
rather than replacing it with new functionality in Emacs; I haven't
surpassed the featureset or implementation of \reftex, certainly not, so
rather than replacing that I'll add to it.

\chapter{Configuring \reftex to support the \noweb through knoweb} \label{cha:conf-reft-recogn}

\begin{quotation}
  Support for RefTeX in such a style file is useful when the LaTeX style
 defines macros or environments connected with labels, citations, or the index.
\end{quotation}

<<knoweb.el>>=
;;; This is knoweb.el --- an Emacs LISP style file for AUCTeX and RefTeX. -*- mode: emacs-lisp -*-
<<knoweb.el>>=
;; AUCTeX customization: support for knoweb label environments.
;; Conditionally supporting RefTeX.
(TeX-add-style-hook "knoweb"
 (lambda ()
   (if (fboundp 'reftex-add-label-environments)
       ;; Provide the symbol for the knoweb label environments.
       (reftex-add-label-environments '(webcode moddef nwtypesetcomment thenowebchunks thenowebindex)))))
@ 

