\chapter{Commands for Human Languages}\label{noweb-narrowing}
The Emacs Manual has a chapter dedicated to /Commands for Human
Languages/. This software documentation shall have a chapter dedicated
to that too, since literate programming (per Knuth) is primarily
intended for Human readers.

\section{Pages}
\begin{quote}
``Emacs provides commands to move over pages and to operate on them.''
\end{quote}

Such is the primary purpose of \whyse{}, to extend this page-handling to
noweb ``chunks'', which may be delimited by adding the following to
the regular expression which normally matches only form-feed
characters.

<<customize page-delimiter regular expression for noweb>>=
(customize-set-variable
 'page-delimiter
 "^\\(@<<.*>>=\\)\\|^\\(@\\)\\|^\f"
 "Modify the default value of the `page-delimiter' such that the
built-in page navigation features are useful in noweb buffers.")
@

Navigating between chunks in a noweb source document is so fundamental
to literate programming that you may want to bind \texttt{backward-page}
and \texttt{forward-page} to the specially labelled "Page Up" and "Page
Down" keys on your keyboard (represented in elisp with the special
strings "<prior>" and "<next>" when you use \texttt{describe-key} on
these keyboard buttons).

\section{Advising the forward- and backward-page functions}
Using the Emacs advice interface is probably the most straightforward
way to implement widening and re-narrowing of the buffer when calling
\texttt{forward-page} and \texttt{backward-page}, and further to echo
the result of \texttt{what-page}.

It would be simplest to not handle the page delimiter specially unless
necessary, so leaving this delimiter in-place is most flexible.

<<nwn.el>>=
;;; Noweb Narrowing -*- mode: emacs-lisp -*-

;;; Commentary:
;;  Advise the `forward-page` and `backward-page` functions to widen and
;;  then restore chunk-based narrowing when it is in effect (if and only
;;  if the current buffer is editing a .nw file).

<<current buffer is a noweb file>>=
(string= "nw" (file-name-extension (buffer-file-name)))
@

After reviewing the function documentation for \texttt{narrow-to-page}
I learned of the effect of the universal argument. The simplest way to
advise the forward and backward page functions to work as desired
would be to provide advise which replaces the function call with a
call to narrow to page with an appropriate numeric argument.

Undesireabley, page-based narrowing hides the code chunk definition
line. It is permissible that it hides the documentation chunk
definition, but it is simpler and more extensible to let these remain
visible but read-only when narrowing is in effect.

<<nwn.el>>=
;;; nwn-narrow-to-page is a modified version of the built-in function
;;; narrow-to-page.
(defun nwn-narrow-to-page (&optional arg)
  "Make text outside current page invisible.

For this modified version of `narrow-to-page', a page is a noweb
chunk (either code or docs). See the `page-delimiter'.

A numeric arg specifies to move forward or backward by that many
chunks, thus showing a chunk other than the one point was
originally in."
  <<declare the function interactivity-type and set arg to zero if missing>>
  (save-excursion
    <<widen the buffer then handle non-zero arg values>>
    @

    After widening the buffer and handling non-zero argument values, it is
    necessary to find the end of the current page (chunk) to establish the
    end of the region in which narrowing will take place (a region delimited
                                                            by chunk boundaries).

    <<nwn.el>>=
    <<find the end of the page>>
    <<restore chunk-narrowing by narrowing to region delimited by chunk delimiters>>))

<<declare the function interactivity-type and set arg to zero if missing>>=
(interactive "P")
(setq arg (if arg (prefix-numeric-value arg) 0))
<<widen the buffer then handle non-zero arg values>>=
(widen)
(if (> arg 0)
    (forward-page arg)
  (if (< arg 0)
      (let ((adjust 0)
            (opoint (point)))
        ;; If we are not now at the beginning of a page, move back one
        ;; extra time, to get to the start of this page.
        (save-excursion
          (beginning-of-line)
          (or (and (looking-at page-delimiter)
                   (eq (match-end 0) opoint))
              (setq adjust 1)))
        (forward-page (- arg adjust)))))
<<find the end of the page>>=
(set-match-data nil)
(forward-page)
@

%% TODO: credit this text, which came from the original source code for
%% narrow-to-page.
\begin{itemize}
\item{If we stopped due to end of buffer, stay there.}
\item{If we stopped after a page delimiter, put end of restriction at the beginning of that line.}
\item{Before checking the match that was found, verify that forward-page actually set the match data.}
\end{itemize}

<<find the end of the page>>=
(if (and (match-beginning 0)
         (save-excursion
           (goto-char (match-beginning 0))
           (looking-at page-delimiter)))
    (goto-char (match-beginning 0)))
<<restore chunk-narrowing by narrowing to region delimited by chunk delimiters>>=
(narrow-to-region (point)
                  (progn
                    ;; Find the top of the page.
                    (forward-page -1)
                    ;; If we found beginning of buffer, stay there.
                    ;; If extra text follows page delimiter on same line,
                    ;; include it.
                    ;; Otherwise, show text starting with following line.
                    (if (and (eolp) (not (bobp)))
                        (forward-line 1))
                    (point)))
@

See these overlay properties: [[insert-in-front-hooks]],
[[modification-hooks]], and [[insert-behind-hooks]]; also see
[[keymap]]. These are sufficient, surprisingly, to implement the
entirety of the "in-buffer" UI that I was imagining that I'd develop
with \textit{widget.el}.

The overlay property [[:before-string]] really helps!

<<nwn.el>>=
(defun nwn--forward-page ()
  "Move forward one chunk."
  (interactive)
  (nwn-narrow-to-page 1)
  (goto-char (point-min))
  (recenter-top-bottom 0))

(defun nwn--backward-page ()
  "Move backward one chunk."
  (interactive)
  (nwn-narrow-to-page -1)
  (goto-char (point-min))
  (recenter-top-bottom 0))

(define-minor-mode nwn-mode
  "Noweb Narrowing minor mode provides alternative motion commands
between pages, defined by a buffer-local value of
`page-delimiter' appropriate for Noweb chunks."
  :lighter " nWn "
  :list '(fundamental-mode text-mode latex-mode noweb-mode poly-noweb-mode)
  :keymap '(([next] . nwn--forward-page)
            ([prior] . nwn--backward-page))
  (if <<current buffer is a noweb file>>
      (if nwn-mode
          (progn (make-local-variable 'page-delimiter)
                 <<customize page-delimiter regular expression for noweb>>
                 (nwn-narrow-to-page))
        (kill-local-variable 'page-delimiter)
        (when (buffer-narrowed-p)
          (widen)))
      (message "Current buffer is not a noweb file; refusing to enable nwn-mode!")))

(defvar nwn--chunk-delimiter-match nil
  "The match data for the delimiter previous to the start of the
currently active narrowed buffer region.")

(defun nwn--make-overlay ()
  "Insert the chunk name (page-delimiter) overlay for a narrowed chunk.

Called when narrowing to a page delimited by the
nwn--chunk-delimiter-match, this function inserts the delimiter
preceeding the chunk (if a code chunk) or the most-recent LaTeX
section name (if any, else \"@\" to indicate a documentation
chunk)."
  (let ((ov (make-overlay 0 0 (current-buffer) t)))
    (overlay-put ov 'before-string (concat nwn--chunk-delimiter-match "
"))
    (overlay-put ov 'face widget-documentation-face)
    ov))

(defvar nwn--overlay (nwn--make-overlay))

(defun nwn--forward-page-advice (&rest r)
  (when (boundp 'nwn--overlay)
    (delete-overlay nwn--overlay))
  (nwn--make-overlay))

(advice-add #'nwn-narrow-to-page
            :after #'nwn--forward-page-advice)
@

\chapter{List of Computer Scientists}
Inspired by the random names that Docker uses to name containers, I
derived a list of names of computer scientists or industry professionals
(less some names) from a ``dynamic list'' on Wikipedia.

$$
10^{10}=10000000000
$$

With just ten lists of ten words each there can be ten billion possible
combinations, but this would be ten names for an identifier. That's too
much.

